{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 55, :comments ["<b>Go to Joseph; what he says to you, you shall do.</b> Rashi explained that he asked them to circumcise themselves. The reason for this is that we find that the foreskin is called disgrace (חֶרְפָּה), as it is written (Genesis 34:14), ״כִּי חֶרְפָּה הִיא לָנוּ לָתֵת אֲחוֹתֵנוּ לְאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר לוֹ עָרְלָה״, and famine is also called disgrace (חֶרְפָּה), as it is said (Ezekiel 36:30), ״וְלֹא תִקְחוּ עוֹד חֶרְפַּת רָעָב בַּגּוֹיִם״. Therefore he reasoned that by removing the disgrace of the foreskin, the disgrace of famine would be removed from them — for the removal of the foreskin is the removal of the superfluous blood that causes the rotting, and the decay of the grain comes as well from the side of the superfluities and the refuse within the grain that cause the rotting and the spoiling. And the merit of the covenant of circumcision benefited Noah too, as it is said (Genesis 6:18), ״וַהֲקִמֹתִי אֶת בְּרִיתִי אִתָּךְ״ — Rashi explained: concerning the grain and the fruits, that they would not rot. And so Joseph answered them that they should circumcise themselves, in response to their saying that they had gathered grain and it had rotted. And this is measure for measure: if a person removes the refuse that is within him, then HaShem will also give the good and remove the refuse that is within the grain itself — which is impossible for a person to remove. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 4a): anyone who lies with a harlot, in the end seeks a loaf of bread and does not find it, as it is said (Proverbs 6:26), ״כִּי בְעַד אִשָּׁה זוֹנָה עַד כִּכַּר לָחֶם״ — for one who lies with an Aramean woman draws his foreskin along. And furthermore, circumcision is a repair of the organ so that he not multiply in licentious relations, and the Egyptians were steeped in lust; therefore they needed this repair in the year of famine. And for this reason it is said of Joseph (Genesis 49:24), וַתֵּשֶׁב בְּאֵיתָן קַשְׁתּוֹ — that he subdued his inclination in the matter of Potiphar's wife; therefore מִשָּׁם רֹעֶה אֶבֶן יִשְׂרָאֵל — for from there he merited that his grain did not rot, and he became a shepherd who fed the stone of Israel, by the merit that he did not shepherd after harlots."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:33:01.425615956Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}